      "Toy breeds — Chihuahuas, Yorkies, Pomeranians, Maltese — need movement disproportionate to their size: thirty to forty-five minutes spread across two or three outings, plus indoor play. They are still dogs, with dog brains and dog needs, and the lap-dog stereotype is responsible for an alarming amount of behavioural and weight-related ill health in small breeds.",
      "Puppies follow the five-minute rule: five minutes of structured walking per month of age, twice a day, until the growth plates close (around twelve to eighteen months depending on size). A four-month-old puppy gets two twenty-minute walks — more than that, particularly on hard surfaces or involving jumping and stairs, can damage growing joints in ways that surface as hip dysplasia or elbow problems years later. Free play in a garden is self-regulating; structured walks are not.",
      "Senior dogs need shorter, gentler, more frequent outings. Three fifteen-minute sniff-walks beat one forty-five-minute march. Soft surfaces (grass, dirt) protect joints. Hydrotherapy or swimming, where available, is the gold standard. The aim shifts from cardiovascular conditioning to maintaining mobility, mental engagement and dignity.",
      "Weather rules apply universally. Above 25°C, all dogs benefit from earlier or later outings; above 28°C, exercise should be brief and shaded. The asphalt test: place the back of your hand on the pavement for seven seconds; if you cannot, it is too hot for paws. In winter, salted pavements cause chemical burns and irritation — booties or paw balm protect, and a rinse on return removes residue.",
      "Mental exercise counts double. A twenty-minute scent-work session — hiding treats around the apartment, or a 'find it' game in the garden — tires a dog more than an hour of fetch. Training sessions, new walking routes, social outings, food puzzles and play with appropriate canine companions all contribute to the daily 'enough'. Many dogs marked as 'difficult to tire out' are not unfit; they are mentally under-stimulated.",
      "Recognise the signs of correct exercise. A well-exercised dog settles within thirty minutes of returning home, sleeps soundly through the night, maintains a healthy weight, and shows no compulsive behaviours (excessive licking, tail chasing, perimeter pacing). Under-exercised dogs show the opposite pattern; over-exercised dogs show stiffness, reluctance to walk, slow recovery, or persistent panting. The dog tells you, if you watch.",
      "When your Zoélys sitter takes over, they match your dog's exact routine — the same loop, the same pace, the same duration, the same enrichment. Consistency is the variable that keeps an anxious dog calm and a high-energy dog tolerable when their primary person is not the one holding the leash.",
